Reasoning first, then JSON.

The user asks for **elevation-resolved water partitioning** across the Bow River headwaters — a 2200 m relief basin (1200–3400 m) in Alberta. The entire scientific value of the request rests on one mechanism: elevation acting through precipitation, temperature, and the rain–snow transition to change how water partitions into ET, snowmelt, runoff, and drainage. That mechanism can only be represented if the forcing itself is elevation-resolved.

Here is the blocking problem: **this domain is outside CONUS**, so NLDAS-2 (the 12 km, elevation-differentiating default) is unavailable. The stack falls back to Qian T62 at ~1.9°, which is a single grid cell far coarser than the entire basin — it is NOT elevation-resolved. Every column I place, whether at 1200 m or 3400 m, would receive essentially identical precipitation and temperature. Stratifying columns by elevation would therefore isolate **soil**, not elevation/climate. Presenting such a design as "elevation-resolved partitioning" would be dishonest — the variation seen would be a soil artifact, and snow/rain-transition physics (the dominant control at these elevations) would be flat across the gradient.

Compounding this, **there are no usable in-domain observations**: SNOTEL is US-only, and the ECCC gauges are not ingested by the stack (and streamflow gauges need routing the single-column stack cannot do anyway). So there is no validation path either.

Conclusion: the question **as posed is infeasible** on the current stack. The honest deliverable is to name the blocker (no elevation-resolved forcing outside CONUS), record the missing capabilities, and note the narrow thing that *could* still be explored (soil-texture control on partitioning under a single representative forcing — a conceptual-style exercise, but that is not what was asked). I will not fabricate an elevation design around uniform forcing.

